{
  "id": "COMPOUND:30153 - PROTEIN:5792",
  "verbalization": "Methyl Lucidenate Q appears to interact with RWLeu4 through a series of molecular pathways involving sialyltransferase activity. The drug targets CMP-N-acetylneuraminate-beta-1,4-galactoside alpha-2,3-sialyltransferase, an enzyme that plays a critical role in the synthesis of sialic acid, a component of glycoproteins and glycolipids. This enzyme is also a target of multiple compounds, including (3aR,6S,6aR)-3,6-bis(3,4,5-trimethoxyphenyl)-1,3,3a,4,6,6a-hexahydrofuro[3,4-c]furan and (1S,3E,4S,5S,6S)-4,5-dihydroxy-3-(3-methylbut-2-enylidene)-7-oxabicyclo[4.1.0]heptan-2-one, which both lead to the inhibition of RWLeu4. The shared target of these compounds suggests a potential mechanism of action involving sialyltransferase inhibition, which could disrupt glycan synthesis and function, potentially affecting cellular processes dependent on sialylation. The consistent interaction between Methyl Lucidenate Q and RWLeu4, through the same target enzyme, supports a mechanistic link that is both biologically plausible and chemically consistent.",
